#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Bounding the subspace dimension for the latest version of Sample-based "
"quantum diagonalization (SQD)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bounding the subspace dimension"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"addons/qiskit-addon-sqd/guides/choose-"
"subspace-dimension.ipynb\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this walkthrough, we will show the effect of the subspace dimension in "
"the [self-consistent configuration recovery "
"technique](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.05068).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*A priori*, we do not know what is the correct subspace dimension to obtain a target level of accuracy. However, we do know that increasing the subspace dimension increases the accuracy of the method. Therefore, we can study the accuracy of the predictions as a function of the subspace dimension.\n"
"Specify the molecule and its properties.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Generate some random bitstrings to proxy QPU samples.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Call SQD with increasing batch sizes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This plot shows that increasing the subspace dimension leads to more "
"accurate results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-addon-"
"sqd/guides/choose-subspace-dimension/extracted-"
"outputs/caffd888-e89c-4aa9-8bae-4d1bb723b35e-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Setting an explicit bound with `max_dim` {#setting-an-explicit-bound-with-"
"max_dim}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In the sweep above, the subspace dimension was controlled only indirectly. "
"`samples_per_batch` sets how many bitstrings are drawn into each batch, but "
"the dimension of the resulting subspace is determined by how many *distinct*"
" spin-alpha and spin-beta strings those bitstrings contain, and the pool of "
"configurations available for subsampling grows from one configuration "
"recovery iteration to the next. That is why the subspace dimensions on the "
"horizontal axis above are irregular numbers that we did not pick.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The `max_dim` argument of `diagonalize_fermionic_hamiltonian` bounds the "
"dimension directly. It limits the number of CI strings retained in each spin"
" sector, and since the subspace is spanned by the Cartesian product of the "
"two sectors, its dimension is the product of the two sector dimensions:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`max_dim=d` limits both sectors to `d` strings, so the subspace dimension is"
" at most `d ** 2`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`max_dim=(d_a, d_b)` limits the spin-alpha and spin-beta sectors separately,"
" so the subspace dimension is at most `d_a * d_b`. When "
"`symmetrize_spin=True`, the two limits must be equal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`max_dim=None`, the default, imposes no limit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"When a batch contains more strings than the limit allows, the least "
"important ones are discarded: the strings passed via "
"`include_configurations` are kept first, followed by the strings carried "
"over from the previous iteration (see `carryover_threshold`), and the "
"remaining slots are filled with the sampled strings in decreasing order of "
"how often they were sampled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Because `max_dim` caps the size of the largest diagonalization that the "
"eigenstate solver will be asked to perform, it is a convenient way to keep "
"the solver within a runtime and memory budget: choose `samples_per_batch` "
"generously, and let `max_dim` decide how large the subspace is allowed to "
"grow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Below, we repeat the study, this time holding `samples_per_batch` fixed and "
"increasing `max_dim`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The subspace dimension is now exactly `max_dim ** 2` in every run: it was "
"chosen rather than discovered. Plotting both sweeps together shows that the "
"accuracy is governed by the subspace dimension itself, whichever argument "
"was used to arrive at it. The two curves need not coincide exactly, though. "
"The `max_dim` runs draw 600 bitstrings per batch and then keep the most "
"frequently sampled strings, so at a given dimension they can retain a better"
" set of configurations than a run that reached that dimension by sampling "
"fewer bitstrings in the first place.\n"
msgstr ""

